{"archetypes":[{"name":"shell-portability","focus_area":"correctness","weight":1,"rationale":"New scripts must satisfy the repo's hard Bash 3.2 portability constraint; array-append syntax, herestrings, and BASH_SOURCE usage in lib-phantom-probe.sh and rebase-checkpoint-probe.sh need targeted scrutiny against macOS system bash.","prompt_body":"Audit every new or modified shell file in this diff strictly for Bash 3.2 portability violations as defined in BASH_AUTHORING.md §3: `declare -A`, `local -n`, `mapfile`/`readarray`, `${var^^}`/`${var,,}`, `&>>`, coprocs. Pay particular attention to `rebase_args+=(…)` array-append forms in `scripts/rebase-checkpoint-probe.sh`, any `<<<` herestrings in `scripts/lib-phantom-probe.sh`, and whether `${BASH_SOURCE[0]}` usage is safe under the macOS system bash. Also check `scripts/test-rebase-checkpoint-probe.sh` and `scripts/test-phantom-probe-with-warn.sh` for harness-only Bash 4+ constructs that would break on macOS CI. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"resource-lifecycle","focus_area":"code-robustness","weight":1,"rationale":"The library and wrapper scripts allocate mktemp files inside set+e/set-e brackets; verifying cleanup on all exit paths—including early return from phantom_probe_with_warn and unexpected wrapper exits—is non-trivial and distinct from general correctness.","prompt_body":"Examine temp-file lifecycle in `scripts/lib-phantom-probe.sh` and `scripts/rebase-checkpoint-probe.sh`. The library allocates `append_combined=$(mktemp)` inside a `set +e` / `set -e` bracket and relies on `rm -f` in both the success and failure arms; verify that every code path through `phantom_probe_with_warn` (including the `phantom` and `unknown` cases and the secondary `_phantom_append_warn_failure` call path) cleans up that temp file and never leaks it if a subcommand exits unexpectedly. In `rebase-checkpoint-probe.sh`, the `trap 'rm -f "$rb_stdout" "$rb_stderr"' EXIT` fires on any exit including the re-exit for unexpected rc; confirm the trap fires before the `exit "$rc"` tail and that both `rb_stdout` and `rb_stderr` are always initialized before the trap is registered. Also check whether `_parse_rebase_error` reads the temp files after the trap has already cleaned them on an early-exit path. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"orchestrator-contract","focus_area":"architecture","weight":1,"rationale":"The SKILL.md prose changes define a new orchestrator-to-wrapper contract for parsing REBASE_OUTCOME/REBASE_ERROR; the Exit-3 and 'Other non-zero exit' routing prose are subtly overlapping because the wrapper emits REBASE_ERROR=unexpected-rc-<n> for both cases before re-exiting with the original code, creating potential ambiguity for the orchestrator.","prompt_body":"Review the updated `## Rebase Checkpoint Macro` section in `skills/implement/SKILL.md` for internal consistency: the 'Exit 3 (REBASE_OUTCOME=failed)' bullet says to check whether `REBASE_ERROR` begins with `unexpected-rc-` and then routes to the 'failed unexpectedly' string, while the 'Other non-zero exit' bullet also describes wrapper re-exit with `REBASE_ERROR=unexpected-rc-<n>`. Determine whether these two branches are mutually exclusive in practice (the wrapper exits 3 for non-conflict rebase failure AND may re-exit with the original code for unexpected rc, but `rc=3` for non-conflict failure is also a normal wrapper path), and whether an orchestrator implementing this prose literally could misclassify a genuine rc=3 non-conflict failure as an unexpected exit. Also check whether the SKILL.md text correctly references 'parse the suffix after `unexpected-rc-` from `REBASE_ERROR`' in both places where that suffix is needed for the bail print, and whether the 'Exit 0' bullet's instruction to check `SKIPPED_ALREADY_PUSHED=true` BEFORE `SKIPPED_ALREADY_FRESH=true` is semantically consistent with the wrapper emitting only one of those keys (not both). Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."}]}
